Boudewijn Waijers wrote:

> That "death drop" indeed makes no sense. Why would it appear out of
> nowhere? It should have been there all along with the monster, and the
> monster should have been able to use it against you.

They way I see it, it's a way to generate some of those items that the 
monsters would have used themselves if they'd had them. If you generate 
them in the monster inventory then it's a race to kill the monster 
before it can use the item. On the other hand if you increase the number 
of items generated on dungeon floor then it's race to get to that item 
before monsters and if they win then it's the same race to kill before 
the item is used.

One could increase the number of items generated underground and 
embedded in the walls and statues, but the number of these items would 
not be dependable on the number and quality of monsters killed.

So let's ratinalize and say that the "death drop" is the items that the 
monster carried but didn't remember having or didn't have time to dig 
out from the deep pockets (or whatever).

They even could be generated when the monster is generated but marked as 
unusable. This way a probing could show them.

